# Agentic Workflow Kit — Pi Adapter

Use the model-agnostic runbooks in `~/.agentic-workflows/`. Pi ships without
sub-agents or plan mode by design — translate workflow instructions into
direct actions you perform yourself with the `read`/`write`/`edit`/`bash`
tools; never pretend to spawn an agent that doesn't exist in this harness.

This file loads automatically (Pi concatenates `~/.pi/agent/AGENTS.md` with any
project-local `AGENTS.md` at startup) — no slash command needed to activate
it. Four Pi Skills give explicit entry points: `/skill:nerve <task>`,
`/skill:agentic-init [new|existing|maintenance]`, `/skill:loop-triage`, and
`/skill:engage <persona> "<task>"` (expert-persona framing — see
`~/.agentic-workflows/expert-personas.md`).

Self-learning (retrieve → judge → distill → consolidate) is **automatic**, not
just invocation-time: the `nerve-learning` extension
(`~/.pi/agent/extensions/nerve-learning/`) hooks Pi's `session_start`,
`tool_execution_start`, and `session_shutdown` events to warm-start, capture,
and consolidate on every session — the same three phases Claude Code's
SessionStart/PostToolUse/Stop hooks run, ported to Pi's extension API instead
of approximated.

## `/nerve` loop contract

When a task arrives from an enabled loop, first read `.agentic/config.yml`,
`loop.constraints_file`, `loop.state_file`, and `loop.run_log`. Respect the
kill-switch and daily budget; at 80% budget, produce a report only. Run
`bash scripts/loop-readiness.sh` if present.

- **L1 / report-only:** inspect and update state/log only; never edit source.
- **L2 / assisted:** work on one bounded root cause in a worktree created with
  `bash scripts/loop-worktree.sh create <run-id>`. Pi has no sub-agent
  primitive, so the maker/checker split runs as **two separate `pi` CLI
  sessions** (e.g. a `pi -p "..."` print-mode invocation for each) — never let
  one session verify its own patch.
- Never push, open/update a PR, merge, deploy, publish, change credentials, or
  mutate a third-party service without the user's explicit approval.
- After three attempts or any ambiguity, protected path, or verifier conflict:
  mark the item blocked, append evidence to the loop log and active epic
  Automation Log, then stop.

For raw tasks, follow `nerve-runbook.md`; for an epic task use the project
workflow. Treat memory as an accelerator and tracked docs as the record. MCP
tools (Context7, etc.) are only reachable if an MCP-bridge extension is
installed and configured in `~/.pi/agent/mcp.json` — otherwise use the
`npx ruflo` / `npx @claude-flow/cli` CLI forms documented in the runbooks.
